#> ─── agentify template: codex-agent.toml.tmpl ─────────────────────────────────
#> Emits: <repo>/.codex/agents/{{SUBAGENT_NAME}}.toml   (Codex — **Native**)
#>        Authority on the bytes: adapters/codex.md §4.5. Verdict on support:
#>        adapters/capabilities.md, row "Subagents".
#>
#> This is the Codex half of one artifact type. The Claude Code half is
#> templates/subagent.md.tmpl — same candidate, same evidence string, same agentify-id, two
#> formats: markdown-with-frontmatter there, TOML here. Codex does NOT read
#> `.claude/agents/*.md`, and Claude Code does NOT read this file. With both targets selected,
#> emit both files from the same candidate and say so in the plan.
#>
#> Filling rules
#>  1. Replace every placeholder — an ALL_CAPS name in double braces. Delete every line starting
#>     with "#>". Plain "#" comments stay: they are the emitted file's own documentation.
#>  2. Keep the five agentify-* header lines verbatim. `verify_artifacts.py` reads
#>     `# agentify-id:` style comment headers in any text file, and a rerun matches on
#>     agentify-id to rewrite this file in place instead of writing a second copy.
#>     adapters/codex.md §4.5 shows the same block at the END of the file; both are legal TOML
#>     and the verifier reads them anywhere. Emit them at the top, where a human sees them —
#>     and NEVER inside the ''' literal below, where they would become prompt text.
#>  3. Whole-file artifact. agentify owns this file; it is never merged. If a file of this name
#>     exists WITHOUT the agentify ID block, do not touch it: write
#>     `{{SUBAGENT_NAME}}.toml.proposed` and record a needs-you item (adapters/codex.md §4.5).
#>  4. TOML only. Bare keys, `=` not `:`, strings always quoted. Emit exactly the keys below —
#>     `name`, `description`, `developer_instructions` are required; `model_reasoning_effort`
#>     and `sandbox_mode` are optional and `model` is deliberately never emitted (a pinned model
#>     goes stale and silently changes the user's session).
#>  5. Build a subagent only for work that needs isolated context, a specialist persona, or
#>     parallelism. Codex subagents ARE real separate threads (verified: 60% of the rollout
#>     files on the research machine are subagent threads), so a candidate whose value is
#>     context isolation passes the evidence test on this target — it is not a substitution.
#>  6. **The one honest loss versus Claude Code: there is no per-agent tool allowlist.** If the
#>     Claude Code candidate carried `tools:`, do not invent an equivalent here. Emit
#>     `sandbox_mode = "read-only"` for any review/analysis agent — the nearest thing Codex has —
#>     and state the loss in the plan's capability notes. Never emit `danger-full-access`.
#>  7. Target length for developer_instructions after filling: 40-120 lines, the same budget as
#>     the markdown body it is built from.
#>  8. No attribution inside `developer_instructions`. The one comment line below is the only
#>     placement — attribution must never reach a generated agent's runtime behaviour.
#>  9. Trust gate: like every repo-scoped Codex artifact, this file is inert until the project
#>     is trusted (`[projects."<abs path>"] trust_level = "trusted"`). agentify never edits
#>     config.toml to set it; the report's manual checklist carries it.
#> 9a. Manifest entry: `"type": "subagent"`, `"path": ".codex/agents/<name>.toml"`. The verifier
#>     reads this file's `# agentify-*` header comments correctly and, for a non-markdown path,
#>     reads its identity from the TOML instead of asking for YAML frontmatter:
#>     `subagent_frontmatter` requires `name`, `description` and `developer_instructions`, and a
#>     filename stem equal to `name`. `subagent_tools` checks what Codex actually has — no
#>     per-agent allowlist (a `tools` key WARNs, because the restriction is silently lost), a
#>     pinned `model` WARNs, and `sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access"` FAILs.
#>     **Expect zero FAILs on a correct file.** If `subagent_frontmatter` ever complains about
#>     missing frontmatter here, the verifier is wrong and this file is not: report it and change
#>     nothing (verification.md §9). Never add a `---` block to satisfy it — Codex does not read
#>     one, and a TOML file that starts with `---` no longer parses.
#> 10. Phase 8 validates this file STATICALLY only — that it parses as TOML, that the three
#>     required keys are present, that the filename stem equals `name`. adapters/codex.md §4.5
#>     records that no read-only probe can prove a newly written agents file is loaded, so the
#>     report's manual checklist must carry the live check: in a Codex session in this repo, ask
#>     the model to spawn `{{SUBAGENT_NAME}}` by name, then check the child rollout's `session_meta`
#>     for `source.subagent.thread_spawn.agent_path` pointing at this file.
#>     This is the whole of phase 8 for a Codex subagent and verification.md §7 says the same thing
#>     in the same words — there is no dry-run half on this target, so nothing is being skipped and
#>     nothing may be reported as if it had run. The verified row reads **"written and statically
#>     valid — loading not tested"**, and "working", "live" or "verified" are all false of an agent
#>     that has never been spawned. On Claude Code the same artifact type does get an automatic
#>     `Task` dry-run when the agent is visible to the session; that difference is a fact about the
#>     two targets, not a gap in this file.

# agentify-id: {{AGENTIFY_ID}}
# agentify-version: 1
# agentify-generated: {{GENERATED_DATE}}
# agentify-evidence: {{EVIDENCE_LINE}}
# Safe to delete or edit. This work was brought to you by Agentic Studio.
#
# {{SUBAGENT_TITLE}}
# Remove: delete this file. Nothing else points at it.

#> name — must equal this file's stem, kebab-case. It is how the main thread spawns the agent.
name = "{{SUBAGENT_NAME}}"

#> description — the routing line, and the only thing the main agent reads when deciding whether
#> to delegate. Third person, under 1024 characters, and it must state WHAT THE SUBAGENT RETURNS,
#> not its personality. Three parts in this order:
#>   (a) what it produces (its output, not its process);
#>   (b) when to delegate, in the words the main thread would be thinking;
#>   (c) a "Does not ..." clause naming the nearest neighbour it must not steal work from.
#> Use a """ basic multi-line string so ordinary quotes inside need no escaping; keep it to a few
#> lines. Shape to copy: "Reviews a diff for correctness, missing tests, and violations of this
#> repo's conventions. Returns a severity-ranked finding list with file:line anchors. Delegate
#> when the user asks to review a branch, a PR, or 'the changes'. Does not edit code."
description = """
{{SUBAGENT_DESCRIPTION}}
"""

#> model_reasoning_effort — optional, one of the effort levels this build accepts ("high" is
#> VERIFIED in real files). Emit ONLY if the interview asked for it; delete the line otherwise.
model_reasoning_effort = "{{MODEL_REASONING_EFFORT}}"

#> sandbox_mode — optional. VERIFIED values in the wild: "read-only", "workspace-write". The
#> binary's enum also carries "danger-full-access" and "external-sandbox"; never emit the former.
#> Emit "read-only" for any agent that only reads and reports. Delete the line if the agent must
#> write and the interview approved that.
sandbox_mode = "{{SANDBOX_MODE}}"

#> developer_instructions — the agent's whole system prompt, and the TOML counterpart of the
#> markdown body in subagent.md.tmpl. Use a ''' literal string: nothing inside is escaped, so
#> backslashes, ${VAR}, quotes and backticks are all safe. The one thing a ''' literal cannot
#> contain is ''' itself — if the body needs it, switch that fenced block to ~~~ or use """ and
#> escape. Keep the same section structure the markdown template uses, because it is what makes
#> a subagent behave consistently in a context the caller cannot see.
developer_instructions = '''
# Role

You are {{ROLE_STATEMENT}}.

You work on {{REPO_NAME}}: {{REPO_ONE_LINER}}. The conventions that matter to you are
{{KEY_CONVENTIONS}}.

Your single job is {{SINGLE_JOB}}. You are done when {{DONE_CONDITION}}.

# Scope boundaries

You must refuse and hand back to the caller when:

#> repeat/trim: 3-6 bullets. Each is a concrete refusal, not a value statement. These exist to
#> stop scope creep in a context the main thread cannot see. On this target they carry extra
#> weight: there is no `tools` allowlist to enforce the boundary mechanically.
- {{REFUSE_1}}
- {{REFUSE_2}}
- {{REFUSE_3}}

You never:

- {{NEVER_1}}
- {{NEVER_2}}
- Commit, push, rebase, force-push, or otherwise change git history.
- Read `.env*` files, credential files, or any secret value.

When a boundary is hit, stop immediately and return the output contract below with
`status: blocked` and the reason. Do not work around it.

# Method

#> repeat/trim: 3-8 numbered imperatives. Name exact paths, exact commands, exact search terms.
#> A subagent that has to improvise its own method returns inconsistent work.
1. {{METHOD_1}}
2. {{METHOD_2}}
3. {{METHOD_3}}
4. {{METHOD_4}}

Budget: {{EFFORT_BUDGET}}. If you exceed it, return what you have with `status: partial` rather
than continuing.

# Output contract

Return exactly this structure and nothing else — no preamble, no restatement of the task:

status: done | partial | blocked
summary: <{{SUMMARY_LENGTH}}>
{{OUTPUT_FIELD_1}}: <{{OUTPUT_FIELD_1_SHAPE}}>
{{OUTPUT_FIELD_2}}: <{{OUTPUT_FIELD_2_SHAPE}}>
evidence:
  - <file:line or command output that supports each claim>
open_questions:
  - <anything the caller must decide; empty list if none>

Rules for the output:

- Every claim carries a `file:line` or a command you actually ran. No claim without evidence.
- Report uncertainty as uncertainty. Never present an inference as an observation.
- Keep it under {{OUTPUT_SIZE_CAP}} — the caller pays for this text in its own context window.
'''
